# A decision dependency now opens its original evidence

Document ID: `reiyah.engine.dependency-trace`. Version: `0.1.0`.
Lifecycle status: `exploratory`. Dated 14 September 2026.

The [offline trace command](../research/perception-annotation-trace/0.1.0/README.md) connects a
checked hypothetical label deletion to its exact annotation record, incident detector rows and
available capture context. It works on both actual benchmark cases. Joint deletions remain one
comparison, with the same predictions, weights, tolerance and matching competition throughout.
This makes an existing mathematical result inspectable through a reusable command. It is not
an independently measured customer benefit or a deployed Console interface.

| Case | Checked dependency | Result after the declared deletion | Source opening |
|---|---|---|---|
| First, two anchors | Six separate single-label witnesses | Each changes +1 to 0, criterion excluded | Six original annotations, six incident detector rows, and 14 exact captures |
| Second, 14 anchors | A two-label witness across anchors and a two-label control within one anchor | Each group changes 2/7 to 0, criterion excluded | Three distinct original annotations, three incident detector rows; eight exact captures available and six requested keyframes outside the supplied package |

The two groups in the second row share a label. The row does not claim either label alone
changes the criterion. In fact no single-label deletion can cross this case's threshold.
An annotation with zero lidar and zero radar points in the original metadata is among the
first case's six dependencies. That preserves a concrete source limitation; zero recorded
points do not prove object absence, a false label, or a correct detector.

## The source chain and its limits

The previous synthetic matching-to-source procedure and the actual annotation packets already
contain the necessary representations. No core, compiler, admission or common-operand schema
extension was needed. The missing piece was following an external local-index witness through
those representations without a new bespoke manual join for each case.

The command checks case identity and complete finite graph, retains world plus anchor plus
graph identity, checks the original reference mapping, and reopens the selected original
annotation, sample, instance and category table records. Literal UTF-8 byte offsets and sizes
are checked separately from zero-based row indices. Original prediction indices are retained
through neutral renamings. The extraction writes exact source record bytes.

The capture path selects the exact nominal sample-data identity and verifies the observation
package and original bytes. All 12 camera captures from the first case have timestamp offsets;
its lidar captures have nominal offset zero. In the second dependency, the first anchor's six
camera keyframes are absent from the supplied package. The consumer reports that absence rather
than choosing nearby images. Its lidar capture is available, and the other anchor has all seven
captures. None of these joins establishes object correspondence, synchronization, an object
locator in pixels/points, a human viewing event or reference admission.

The private outputs are under
`~/.codex/reports/reiyah/engine-dependency-trace-2026-09-14-3_e7tw_w/private/`:
`trace-first-reviewed/OPEN_EVIDENCE.md` and `trace-second-reviewed/OPEN_EVIDENCE.md`.
The accompanying trace JSON, original record files and final packet manifest retain exact
source identities. Benchmark identifiers, record payloads and images remain private.

## Consumer review of Fable's second result

The selected sealed outbox has manifest
`184ec42921f46f8403bbf48657d8774542d1344f6ec0b4e5258fdf582295a0e3`, source
`762e1aae2f9dfe6df0808f735d5276dc9d7d963a`, tree
`521f18037392aad226f8bb6ae4e1e2d00085aa80`. All 20 payloads verify, including 16 committed
payloads checked against their exact Git blobs. It responds to the Engine's second-case exchange.

The Engine recomputation checks all 737 single deletions: every criterion remains supported,
delta ranges from 1/7 to 2/7, and the same 53 labels reduce the gain. Both supplied crossing
pairs reproduce zero. Minimality follows from a simple bound: deleting one object changes
each maximum matching by zero or one, so the difference changes by at most one. Its weighted
loss effect is at most `(a+b)*weight`. Here that is 1/7, below the 13/70 margin to tolerance.
One deletion cannot cross, and a checked two-label group does. The minimum is therefore two
under this deletion family; exhaustive pair enumeration is unnecessary for that claim.

Both analysts can use this bound and the same witness. The first and second cases attain their
respective lower bounds of one and two deletions. This is a scoped sensitivity result on the
same two exposed scenes, not an industry result, a label error probability, or evidence that
the detector merits physical deployment.

Fable's repaired geometric insertion rule passes the retained topology/distance counterexample
and refuses an empty coordinate map. A new bounded control finds a remaining defect: if one
other same-class detection is absent from a nonempty coordinate map, its edge is silently
omitted. With detections at 2 and 3 metres and insertion at 3, complete coordinates connect
both; omitting the first coordinate silently connects only the second. This synthetic control
does not invalidate the actual first-case insertion results, whose complete coordinates were
checked. The Engine reports it to Fable rather than editing the research implementation.

Two reporting limits also remain. Fable's preregistration says it preceded the case's existence;
the Engine's result completed at 16:28:05 UTC, before the preregistration's recorded 16:45:20 UTC.
Fable's statement that it had not received the case is a different, lane-reported exposure claim.
Also, the preregistration lists a second-case insertion family, while the selected outbox contains
only the corrected first-case insertion family. An executed result or explicit amendment is
needed to account for that planned item. Neither issue changes the checked deletion arithmetic.

## First failures, validation and effort

The initial single-label trace worked. The first two-label attempt rejected Fable's decimal
cost field as an Engine operand. The correction reads external research reports with the
existing strict source JSON reader while retaining rational-only Engine inputs. The failed
command and source are retained. A later adversary targeted an unavailable camera and wrongly
expected an identity exception; explicit unavailability was the correct result. The corrected
adversary targets an available lidar capture, with missing capture tested separately.

Final source review also reproduced a missing class-binding check in the new consumer: an
altered class on an unselected exported detection passed. The correction checks every exported
detection and object class against its bound normalization or reference record. The original
control, repaired source, all three refusal controls and fresh actual replays are retained.

The first measurement-suite and research-consistency runs found an existing em-dash table
placeholder in the first annotation document. That document is corrected without changing
its numerical result or weakening the checks. Final validation and exact receipts are recorded
in [verification](../research/perception-annotation-trace/0.1.0/verification.json).

Final source trace commands took 25.933 and 26.098 seconds, respectively, running concurrently.
They include reopening the original metadata archive, literal record verification, matching
checks, capture-byte checks and output creation. They are not a comparison against manual
review. Original implementation, preparation, interpretation and repair effort outside command
captures remains unmeasured. No human time or cost saving is claimed.

The next Engine falsifier is whether another consumer can follow a selected joint dependency
to these same original records while detecting missing or substituted evidence. A Console
integration should consume these existing artifacts rather than invent another reference model.
Across-scene validation and measured workflow value remain separate next milestones. Original
physical references stay open at [-8,8]; Gate A is unaccepted and no reviewer task is assigned.
